==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|civilitas}}: compare {{cog|fr|civilité}}. See {{m|en|civil}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/sɪˈvɪl.ɪ.ti/}}
{{rfap|en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|civilities}}

# Speech or behaviour that is fit for [[civil]] interactions; [[politeness]], [[courtesy]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{rfdatek|en|Chesterfield}}
#*: The insolent '''civility''' of a proud man is, if possible, more shocking than his rudeness could be.
# {{lb|en|chiefly|in plural}} An individual act or expression of polite [[behaviour]]; a [[courtesy]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#*'''1748''', {{w|Samuel Richardson}}, ''Clarissa'', I.3:
#*:Mr Lovelace received from every one those '''civilities''' which were due to his birth […].
# {{lb|en|now|archaic}} The state or fact of being [[civilized]]; [[civilization]]. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Davies Discoverie}}
#*: Monarchies have risen from barbarism to '''civility''', and fallen again to ruin.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[civil]] [[office]]; a civil [[capacity]]. {{defdate|16th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Latimer Edward2}}
#*: To serve in a '''civility'''.

